---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const LOG = "server/src/iki_server/audit/log.py";
const AM = "server/src/iki_server/audit/models.py";
const R = "server/src/iki_server/core/db_roles.py";

const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: "An append-only log, held by the database" },
  { id: "how", label: "What's audited, and how" },
  { id: "roles", label: "The migrator and the app" },
  { id: "rules", label: "The rules in full" },
  { id: "ref", label: "Files and decisions" },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title="An append-only log, held by the database">
    <p>
      Every action Iki audits is an <code>AuditEvent</code>, written in the same transaction as the
      change it describes, so it exists exactly when the change does. Once the transaction commits,
      the event is also written to the <code>iki.audit</code> log stream, so a SIEM keeps its own
      copy. No role can change or delete an event: a trigger refuses UPDATE, DELETE and TRUNCATE,
      and the app connects as a role that may only insert and read them. A record's own changes are
      its revisions, which are append-only the same way (<Adr n="0025" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title="What's audited, and how">
    <p>
      <Path p={LOG} sym="def record(" /> refuses an action that isn't in <Path p={LOG} sym="ACTIONS" />,
      the list of every action with how the audit page describes it. An event keeps the action, the
      actor and their email at the time, the channel (<code>via</code>), the target and its name at
      the time, the project or team, details (never secrets), the request ID, the client's address,
      the OAuth client, and the agent, if one acted for the person (<Path p={AM} sym="class AuditEvent(" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      For records, the audit log has what isn't a value: states set (<code>entity.state_changed</code>,
      with both states, the revision and the reason), holds and their reviews, sharing into and out of
      projects, assignments, moves to a superseding entry, amount movements, exports, and every step
      of signing. A plain create or edit writes no audit event: its revision is its trail, with who,
      how, which agent and why (<PageLink to="Revisions and history" />). A record's Audit trail tab
      and its CSV read its revisions, and downloading the CSV is itself audited (<code
        >entity.trail_exported</code
      >).
    </p>
    <p>The channel a change came through:</p>
    <Excerpt file={AM} start="^class Via\(" />
    <p>
      Auditors read the log at <code>/admin/audit/</code> and export it as CSV (<Path
        p="server/src/iki_server/audit/views.py"
      />). What each event, webhook and notification tells whom is on <PageLink to="What events disclose" />.
    </p>

    <h3 id="roles">The migrator and the app</h3>
    <p>
      Postgres has two roles for Iki (<Path p={R} />). The <strong>migrator</strong> owns every table
      and is used only by the Helm migrate Job, to run <code>migrate_deployment</code>, which grants
      the app its privileges in the transaction that migrates the plugins (<Adr n="0134" />). The{" "}
      <strong>app</strong> role, used by web pods and maintenance jobs, reads and writes rows but
      can't create, alter or drop anything. Its grants (<Path p={R} sym="def grant(" />):
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>no CREATE on the schema, and no TRUNCATE, REFERENCES or TRIGGER on any table;</li>
      <li>no UPDATE or DELETE on any append-only table, every model with <code>APPEND_ONLY = True</code>: the audit log and its agents, revisions, every <code>__history</code> table, signatures, the amounts ledger's movements, and more (<Path p={R} sym="def append_only_tables(" />);</li>
      <li>read only on the graph's edges, kept history changes, Django's and plugins' migration history, plugins' states, and every table of a detached plugin (<Path p={R} sym="READ_ONLY_TABLES" />).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A web pod whose role is too powerful never turns ready: the readiness check fails if the role is
      a superuser, can create roles or databases, bypasses row-level security, can create in the
      schema, owns tables, or can change an append-only or read-only table (<Path
        p={R}
        sym="def problems("
      />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/health.py" sym="def check_database_role(" />). Granting
      privileges refuses to run when the two roles are the same (<Path
        p="server/src/iki_server/core/management/commands/grant_app_privileges.py"
        sym="The app and the migrator are both"
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title="The rules in full">
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Triggers hold for the owner too" open>
        <p>
          <code>iki_refuse_audit_change()</code> refuses UPDATE and DELETE on each row and TRUNCATE on
          the table, for <code>iki_audit_event</code> and <code>iki_audit_agent</code>, whatever the
          role (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/audit/migrations/0001_initial.py" />). Revisions,
          history tables, signatures and the ledger get the same from the <code>AppendOnly</code>{" "}
          migration operation (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/plugins/operations.py" sym="class AppendOnly" />).
          The grants keep the app from trying; the triggers hold even for the migrator outside a
          migration.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Agents are stored once">
        <p>
          Each distinct agent is one append-only <code>Agent</code> row, which events and revisions
          point to: <PageLink to="Revisions and history" hash="agents" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0025" /> lists the audit channels as web, API, MCP, command line or system; the
            code adds <code>scim</code> and <code>trigger</code>, from <Adr n={[26, 80]} />.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title="Files and decisions">
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/audit" />: the log, its page and agents</li>
          <li><Path p={R} />: the app role's grants and checks</li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/trails.py" />: a record's trail</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0025" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0072" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0134" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
